You can create a class called "Stack" to represent a stack data structure by defining its methods and attributes. Here's an example:

```python
class Stack:
    def __init__(self):
        self.stack = []

    def is_empty(self):
        return self.stack == []

    def push(self, data):
        self.stack.append(data)

    def pop(self):
        data = self.stack[-1]
        del self.stack[-1]
        return data

    def peek(self):
        return self.stack[-1]

    def size_stack(self):
        return len(self.stack)
```

This class has an attribute called "stack" which is initialized as an empty list when an instance of the class is created. The "is_empty" method checks whether the stack is empty or not. The "push" method adds an element to the top of the stack. The "pop" method removes and returns the element at the top of the stack. The "peek" method returns the element at the top of the stack without removing it. The "size_stack" method returns the number of elements in the stack.

You can use this class to create stack objects and perform stack operations.

